  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 249 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A widespread light rainfall event remains on track for \ntoday. There has been a slight trend towards less rainfall and \nshorter duration of rain, as well as lower chances for rain in the \nSt. Lawrence Valley. That being said, the forecast generally is \npretty consistent with observations so far early this morning. \nMoisture advection from the south and southwest, particularly from \nthe surface up to 700 millibars, is occurring with a sharp gradient, \nwhile westerly mid-level/upper level is present. An inverted trough \nin northern New York and weak surface low sliding eastward across \nnorthern Pennsylvania will support modest southeasterly winds across \nthe Champlain Valley and points east. \n\nStratiform rain will blossom in northern New York and gradually\nexpand east-northeastward this morning. During the afternoon, \nthe area of rain will contract from west to east and towards the\nsoutheast as the 850 millibar trough axis and associated low \nlevel convergence and lift exits. Rainfall rates will be 0.01 - \n0.05\" for most of the event, but up to a few hours intensity \nwill likely increase and reach the 0.1\"-0.15\" range (typically \nconsidered \"moderate\" rather than light). These moderate \nrainfall rates are favored from the Adirondacks eastward over \nmost of northern Vermont at times from about 10 AM and 3 PM. As \na result, event totals, as they would be for a winter weather \nevent, are somewhat uncertain but should be mostly in the 0.25\" \nto 0.5\" range within much of the region. \n\nTemperatures today will generally be chilly in the low to mid 50s \nwith thick clouds and rain for much of the day, and even cooler in \nportions of central/eastern Vermont due to the aforementioned \nupslope easterly component to the wind. It is a tricky hourly \ntemperature forecast with large dependence on rainfall timing; \nas a result, there is potential for late day highs where rain \nends early enough and morning highs in northern and eastern \nportions of Vermont prior to rain arriving.\n \nTonight we'll lock in some low clouds and patchy fog ahead of a\nmoisture starved cold front arriving from the northwest. Once \nthis boundary passes through, northerly flow will develop with\nsome low clouds slow to clear out, especially in the vicinity \nof the higher terrain with moist 925-850 millibar air expected\nto linger into the day. Clouds should gradually scatter\ngenerally from north to south, leading to the beginning of a\nprolonged stretch of primarily clear skies. Fog chances in climo\nfavored areas will begin high Tuesday, but tend to decrease \neach morning following days of drying after Sunday's rainfall.\nTemperatures will remain below normal through Wednesday with the\ndominance of strong high pressure to our north supplying us \nwith seasonably cool air.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: The blocking pattern will remain in place much of next \nweek, with high pressure centered to our north while low pressure \ndevelops off the Mid Atlantic. While our weather will be pleasant, \ntemperatures will likely remain below normal, with daytime highs \nmainly in the 60s. Clear skies and light winds will allow for chilly \novernights, and patchy frost will be possible in the \nAdirondacks/eastern VT valleys each morning. While confidence has \nincreased in the dry weather persisting through Thursday, low \npressure meandering around off the East Coast will keep the threat \nof showers around as we head toward the weekend, especially in \nsouthern/eastern VT. Have stayed with the NBM/WPC forecast for \nFriday onward given the spread in model solutions for mid/late next \nweek.",
